{
  "case_id": "2007-07-440",
  "document_hash": "21a6ec4c1bb1def02b67d090cde6c98124d981b808a96654d8f2fb1e15e90e8c",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Danielle Spinelli",
      "case_id": "2007-07-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "21a6ec4c1bb1def02b67d090cde6c98124d981b808a96654d8f2fb1e15e90e8c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-21a6ec4c1bb1-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We don't contend that it is adversarial."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Coleman",
      "case_id": "2007-07-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "21a6ec4c1bb1def02b67d090cde6c98124d981b808a96654d8f2fb1e15e90e8c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-21a6ec4c1bb1-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "1 demanding one?\n2 MR. COLEMAN: Because there is no prejudice\n3 to your fair trial rights from not choosing to have an\n4 examining trial. We are unaware of any case that has\n5 said that there is a Sixth Amendment right to consult\n6 with counsel before deciding whether to ask for an\n7 examining trial. And in fact, in Texas they are very\n8 rare because in the very unusual circumstances where\n9 somebody asks for one, more often than not the\n10 prosecutor will simply hurry up and do an indictment.\n11 And so there will be no examining trial that takes\n12 place. And that's a put up or shut up procedure, but\n13 it's not something that prejudices your fair trial\n14 rights if no examining trial actually takes place."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2007-07-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "21a6ec4c1bb1def02b67d090cde6c98124d981b808a96654d8f2fb1e15e90e8c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-21a6ec4c1bb1-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "15 JUSTICE KENNEDY: If we said that when a\n16 defendant is ordered held in custody, that there is then\n17 a right of counsel, would we be contradicting any of our\n18 precedents as opposed, say, to extending them?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2007-07-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "21a6ec4c1bb1def02b67d090cde6c98124d981b808a96654d8f2fb1e15e90e8c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-21a6ec4c1bb1-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "5 JUSTICE BREYER: Suppose you were to say\n6 that where the State arrests an individual, brings him\n7 before a neutral official and intends and does impose a\n8 significant restraint on his liberty for the purpose of\n9 bringing that individual to trial, there is a\n10 presumption that that -- forget the presumption. If\n11 that happens for the purpose, the primary purpose,\n12 primary purpose of bringing the individual to trial, at\n13 that point the Sixth Amendment right attaches.\n14 Now, the State would not have to give him a"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Coleman",
      "case_id": "2007-07-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "21a6ec4c1bb1def02b67d090cde6c98124d981b808a96654d8f2fb1e15e90e8c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-21a6ec4c1bb1-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "1 MR. COLEMAN: I -- I think the primary issue\n2 with that, Justice Breyer, is that it contradicts what\n3 the Court said in Gouveia. The Court went through a\n4 lengthy exegesis in Gouveia about what interests in\n5 particular are protected and talked about this, this\n6 expression of a concern about our liberty interests; and\n7 that the purpose issue was not something that the Court\n8 addressed there."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2007-07-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "21a6ec4c1bb1def02b67d090cde6c98124d981b808a96654d8f2fb1e15e90e8c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-21a6ec4c1bb1-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "12 JUSTICE SOUTER: And that would be subject,\n13 wouldn't it, to sort of a condition subsequent, because\n14 if it turned out -- I mean we -- no counsel is required\n15 if there's no incarceration."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Amicus",
      "case_id": "2007-07-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "21a6ec4c1bb1def02b67d090cde6c98124d981b808a96654d8f2fb1e15e90e8c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-21a6ec4c1bb1-0002-0005",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "12 MR. COLEMAN: That amicus brief attempts to\n13 suggest that Texas's statute is very different from\n14 statutes that exist in other cases -- in other States,\n15 and that's simply not true, Your Honor.\n16 I do not understand what happens in each of\n17 those -- in each of those States, but I have at least\n18 seen decisions in some of those States that suggest that\n19 they don't act that much differently than we do, and\n20 that -- that they do apply a critical-stage-type"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Coleman",
      "case_id": "2007-07-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "21a6ec4c1bb1def02b67d090cde6c98124d981b808a96654d8f2fb1e15e90e8c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-21a6ec4c1bb1-0002-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "3 MR. COLEMAN: I think if -- if he were to\n4 ask somebody, he would be told that you can ask for an\n5 examining trial. If you ask for that, we will appoint\n6 you a lawyer; and you will have your examining trial\n7 unless the State decides to indict before we actually\n8 get to it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2007-07-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "21a6ec4c1bb1def02b67d090cde6c98124d981b808a96654d8f2fb1e15e90e8c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-21a6ec4c1bb1-0002-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "15 JUSTICE GINSBURG: Is it then episodic?\n16 That is, if the right is turned on when there is a\n17 critical event, and then the critical event is over, and\n18 no more right to counsel until the next critical event?\n19 It's not -- you have no right to counsel in between\n20 those critical events?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2007-07-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "21a6ec4c1bb1def02b67d090cde6c98124d981b808a96654d8f2fb1e15e90e8c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-21a6ec4c1bb1-0002-0010",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "8 JUSTICE KENNEDY: Can you give me -- can you\n9 give me some idea, or some empirical assessment, or tell\n10 me where I could go to find out, as we sit here, how\n11 many people are being held in custody after a\n12 probable-cause determination and do not have counsel\n13 appointed for them and do not have the right to have\n14 counsel appointed to them until some other critical\n15 phase takes place?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2007-07-440",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "21a6ec4c1bb1def02b67d090cde6c98124d981b808a96654d8f2fb1e15e90e8c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-21a6ec4c1bb1-0002-0011",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "4 JUSTICE SOUTER: At that point, under Texas\n5 law, the right to counsel within one or three business\n6 days would have attached?"
    },
